        <pattern id="dot-pattern-ellipticalvstreadmillcalories" x="0" y="0" width="24" height="24" patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse">
          <circle cx="2" cy="2" r="1.5" fill="white"></circle>
        </pattern>
        <rect width="100%" height="100%" fill="url(#dot-pattern-ellipticalvstreadmillcalories)"></rect>
      </svg>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center gap-3 mb-6 flex-wrap">
          <span class="bg-[#FF5A1F] text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Question Answered
          </span>
          <span class="bg-green-500 text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Updated October 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          What Burns More Calories: <span class="text-[#5AA9FF]">Elliptical or Treadmill?</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 max-w-2xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
          At the same effort, the two machines burn about the same. The difference comes from how hard people actually work on each: running on a treadmill is a little more costly than even a vigorous elliptical session, while a comfortable elliptical session burns about what a brisk treadmill walk does. Here is the arithmetic for three body weights, and why the console figures disagree.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-4">
          <a href="#short-answer" class="bg-[#FF5A1F] hover:bg-[#E64F16] text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm">
            The Short Answer
          </a>
          <a href="#calorie-tables" class="bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm border border-white/20">
            Calorie Tables
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>


    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4 py-8 border-b border-gray-100">
        <img src="/authors/shaheer-ali-khan.png" alt="Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD" width="48" height="48" class="w-12 h-12 rounded-full" />
        <div>
          <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 leading-tight"><a href="/editorial-standards/#who" rel="author" class="text-gray-900 no-underline hover:underline">Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD</a></p>
          <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm leading-tight">Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist · ACSM-EP, CSCS</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block w-px h-8 bg-gray-200"></div>
        <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm">
          Last Updated: <strong class="text-gray-700">October 18, 2026</strong>
        </p>
        <div class="ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="1.4k" />
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="not-prose my-10 bg-blue-50 border border-blue-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-700 m-0 leading-relaxed">
          <strong class="text-gray-900">What this page is, and is not.</strong> We review exercise
          equipment. This is a comparison of estimated energy cost on two machines — it is not
          medical, nutrition or coaching advice, and we are not qualified to give any of those. If
          you have a heart, lung, metabolic or joint condition, take medication that affects heart
          rate, are pregnant, or have a history of disordered eating, talk to a doctor or a
          registered dietitian before using calorie figures to plan exercise or eating.
          <strong class="text-gray-900">On the figures below:</strong> MET values were checked on
          18 October 2026 against the 2024 Adult Compendium of Physical Activities (Herrmann and
          colleagues, <em>Journal of Sport and Health Science</em>, 2024, with the full lists at
          pacompendium.com) and its published tracking guide. Calorie figures are our arithmetic
          from those values — calories ≈ MET × body weight in kg × hours — rounded to the nearest
          5. They are estimates for an average adult, not measurements, and real individuals can
          differ from them meaningfully.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <h2 class="flex items-center gap-2 text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mt-0 mb-4">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-5 h-5 text-[#FF5A1F]" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M4 6h16M4 12h16M4 18h7"></path></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#short-answer" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">1. What Burns More Calories, Elliptical or Treadmill? The Short Answer</a>
          <a href="#compendium" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">2. What Does the Compendium Say About Each Machine?</a>
          <a href="#calorie-tables" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">3. Elliptical vs Treadmill Calories by Body Weight</a>
          <a href="#same-effort" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">4. Do They Burn the Same at the Same Effort?</a>
          <a href="#perceived-effort" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">5. Why Does the Elliptical Feel Easier?</a>
          <a href="#consoles" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">6. Why Do Console Calorie Figures Disagree and Run High?</a>
          <a href="#impact" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">7. Impact and Joints: The Trade Behind the Numbers</a>
          <a href="#weight-loss" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">8. What Does "Burns More" Mean for Weight Loss?</a>
          <a href="#which-to-use" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">9. So Which Should You Use?</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">10. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          "Which burns more" sounds like a question about the machines, but the machines barely
          matter. What decides the calories is how hard you work and for how long, multiplied by how
          much you weigh. The treadmill and the elliptical differ in how easy they make it to work
          hard, how easy they make it to coast without noticing, and how honest their consoles are
          about the result. This page works through the published energy costs, shows what they mean
          for three body weights, explains why the elliptical feels easier and its console often
          reads higher, and puts the whole question in its proper place for weight loss.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. What Burns More Calories, Elliptical or Treadmill? The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Running on a treadmill burns somewhat more than a vigorous elliptical session,
            and a moderate elliptical session burns about the same as a brisk treadmill walk — so the
            treadmill wins at the top end, but at matched effort the difference is small.</strong> In
            the 2024 Compendium of Physical Activities, elliptical training is rated at 5.0 METs for
            moderate effort and 9.0 METs for vigorous effort; treadmill walking at 3.5 to 3.9 mph is
            4.8 METs and running at 6 mph is 9.3.
          </p>
          <p>
            For a 160 lb person, that works out at roughly 180 calories for 30 minutes of moderate
            elliptical work against roughly 175 for a brisk treadmill walk, and about 330 for 30
            vigorous elliptical minutes against about 340 for a 30-minute run at 6 mph. Steep
            treadmill walking sits in between, at around 255. Those are estimates from published
            averages, labelled as such throughout this page.
          </p>
          <p>
            The practical answer is that the machine you will work hard on, regularly, burns more.
            Many people find the elliptical lets them work longer with less discomfort; many others
            find it lets them drift into an easy pace without realising. Both machines' consoles
            tend to overstate what they report. If you are choosing between the two machines for
            reasons beyond calories — joints, space, noise, running goals — our full
            <a href="/treadmill-vs-elliptical/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill vs elliptical comparison</a>
            covers which is best for which person.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="compendium" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. What Does the Compendium Say About Each Machine?</h2>
<p>
            The Compendium of Physical Activities is the standard reference for the energy cost of
            everyday activities and exercise. Its 2024 adult edition, the third update since 1993,
            lists more than a thousand activities, each with a code and a MET value: the activity's
            energy cost as a multiple of resting energy use. Sitting quietly is 1 MET by definition.
            If METs are new to you, our explainer on
            <a href="/what-are-mets-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">what METs are on a treadmill</a>
            covers the idea and the equations consoles use.
          </p>
          <p>
            These are the entries relevant to this comparison, as listed in the 2024 Compendium on the
            date we checked:
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead><tr>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Compendium code</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Activity</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">METs</th>
            </tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">17355</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Walking, treadmill, 3.0 to 3.4 mph, 0% grade</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">3.8</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">17358</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Walking, treadmill, 3.5 to 3.9 mph, 0% grade</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">4.8</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">02048</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Elliptical trainer, moderate effort</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">5.0</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">17034</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Climbing hills, no load, 1 to 5% grade, moderate-to-brisk pace</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">5.3</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">17035</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Climbing hills, no load, 6 to 10% grade, moderate-to-brisk pace</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">7.0</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">12030</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Running, 5.0 to 5.2 mph (12 min/mile)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">8.5</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">02049</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Elliptical trainer, vigorous effort</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">9.0</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">12050</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Running, 6 to 6.3 mph (10 min/mile)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">9.3</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">12070</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Running, 7 mph (8.5 min/mile)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">11.0</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Three caveats belong with that table. First, the Compendium does not define "moderate"
            and "vigorous" elliptical effort in terms of resistance level or strides per minute, and
            no such definition could work across machines, because resistance levels are not
            standardised between brands. Our reading, used throughout this page, is that moderate
            means working steadily while still able to talk in sentences, and vigorous means only a
            few words at a time.
          </p>
          <p>
            Second, the 2024 Compendium lists treadmill walking only at 0% grade. The incline rows
            above are its "climbing hills" entries, which are not treadmill-specific, so we use them
            as a reasonable stand-in. They agree well with the ACSM walking equation that our
            <a href="/treadmill-calories-burned/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill calories burned</a>
            guide uses: 3 mph at a 10% grade works out at a little over 7 METs that way.
          </p>
          <p>
            Third, Compendium values and the ACSM equations do not agree exactly at every speed. For
            running at 6 mph, for example, the equation gives slightly over 10 METs where the
            Compendium lists 9.3. Differences of that size are a fair indication of the precision any
            of these figures has: good to within about 10%, not to the calorie.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="calorie-tables" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. Elliptical vs Treadmill Calories by Body Weight</h2>
<p>
            The arithmetic is simple: <strong>calories ≈ METs × body weight in kilograms ×
            hours</strong>. It rests on the convention that 1 MET costs about one calorie per kilogram
            per hour, which is close enough for estimates. The table below applies it to a 30-minute
            block at each intensity for three body weights. All figures are rounded estimates.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead><tr>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">30 minutes of…</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">METs</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">~130 lb (59 kg)</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">~160 lb (73 kg)</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">~200 lb (91 kg)</th>
            </tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Treadmill walk, 3.0–3.4 mph, flat</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">3.8</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">~110</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">~140</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">~175</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Treadmill walk, 3.5–3.9 mph, flat</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">4.8</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">~140</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">~175</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">~220</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Elliptical, moderate effort</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">5.0</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">~150</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">~180</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">~230</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Incline walk, 1–5% grade, brisk</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">5.3</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">~155</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">~195</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">~240</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Incline walk, 6–10% grade, brisk</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">7.0</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">~205</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">~255</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">~320</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Treadmill run, 5.0–5.2 mph</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">8.5</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">~250</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">~310</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">~385</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Elliptical, vigorous effort</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">9.0</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">~265</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">~330</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">~410</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Treadmill run, 6.0–6.3 mph</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">9.3</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">~275</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">~340</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">~425</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Treadmill run, 7 mph</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">11.0</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">~325</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">~400</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">~500</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            For an hour, double the figures: a 160 lb person comes out at roughly 365 calories for an
            hour of moderate elliptical work, 655 for an hour of vigorous elliptical work and 680 for
            an hour of running at 6 mph — though very few beginners can run or work vigorously for a
            full hour. For a body weight not in the table, multiply your weight in kilograms (pounds
            divided by 2.2) by the MET value and by the hours.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Gross and net calories</h3>
          <p>
            These are gross figures: they include the energy you would have used anyway by sitting
            still for the same half hour, about 1 MET. To see what the session added, subtract 1 from
            the MET value before multiplying. For a 160 lb person, 30 minutes of moderate elliptical
            work then comes to about 145 extra calories, a brisk treadmill walk about 140, vigorous
            elliptical work about 290 and a 6 mph run about 305. The net figure is the honest one to
            use if you are thinking about food.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What the tables show</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Within an intensity band, the machines are close.</strong> Moderate
            elliptical work and a brisk treadmill walk are within a few percent; vigorous elliptical
            work and steady running at 6 mph are within a few percent.</li>
            <li><strong>Intensity matters far more than machine.</strong> Moving from moderate to
            vigorous on the same elliptical nearly doubles the burn. Switching machines at the same
            effort changes it by a few percent.</li>
            <li><strong>Incline walking closes much of the gap to running.</strong> A brisk walk at 6
            to 10% sits about halfway between a moderate elliptical session and a run, without
            running's impact. Our comparison of
            <a href="/incline-walking-vs-running/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">incline walking vs running</a>
            goes further into that trade.</li>
            <li><strong>Body weight multiplies everything.</strong> The 200 lb column is about half
            as much again as the 130 lb column on every row, on either machine.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div id="same-effort" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Do They Burn the Same at the Same Effort?</h2>
<p>
            Broadly, yes. A calorie burned is a calorie burned, and at the same oxygen consumption the
            energy cost is the same whichever machine produced it. The Compendium's vigorous
            elliptical value of 9.0 METs and its 6 mph running value of 9.3 are close enough that the
            difference is within the error of the figures themselves.
          </p>
          <p>
            One way to see it is to ask how long each takes to burn the same amount. For a 160 lb
            person, using the same Compendium values, reaching about 250 gross calories takes
            roughly:
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[420px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead><tr>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Activity (160 lb)</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Minutes to ~250 calories</th>
            </tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Treadmill walk, 3.0–3.4 mph, flat</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">~54</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Treadmill walk, 3.5–3.9 mph, flat</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">~43</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Elliptical, moderate effort</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">~41</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Incline walk, 6–10% grade</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">~29</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Elliptical, vigorous effort</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">~23</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Treadmill run, 6.0–6.3 mph</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">~22</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            The real difference between the machines is not the cost of a given effort but which
            efforts people reach and sustain on each. A treadmill's top end is high: a runner at 7 or
            8 mph is above anything the Compendium credits to an elliptical. The elliptical's
            strength is the middle: many people can work at a steady moderate-to-hard effort for
            longer on it, because it does not hurt the way running sometimes does. Forty-five
            minutes of hard elliptical work outburns twenty minutes of running that leaves your
            knees complaining, and a run you do outburns an elliptical session you skip.
          </p>
          <p>
            The same logic applies to the other machines people weigh up against a treadmill. The
            Compendium lists a stair treadmill ergometer at 9.3 METs, in the same band as running at
            6 mph; our
            <a href="/stairmaster-vs-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">StairMaster vs treadmill</a>
            comparison covers what that means in practice, and our
            <a href="/treadmill-vs-exercise-bike/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill vs exercise bike</a>
            page does the same for cycling.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="perceived-effort" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Why Does the Elliptical Feel Easier?</h2>
<p>
            Because several things about the elliptical lower the effort you feel, and some of them
            also lower the effort you actually make.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>No impact.</strong> Your feet never leave the pedals, so there is no landing
            shock and none of the jarring that makes running feel hard. The heart and lungs can be
            working just as hard as on a run, but the session feels smoother. That is a genuine
            advantage, and it is also why people misjudge their effort on an elliptical.</li>
            <li><strong>Momentum.</strong> The flywheel keeps the pedals moving between pushes. At
            low resistance, momentum does a lot of the work, and you can keep a respectable stride
            rate going while doing much less than it looks. A treadmill belt offers no such help: at
            6 mph, you run at 6 mph or you step off.</li>
            <li><strong>The handles.</strong> Moving handles spread the work across arms and legs,
            which can make a given total effort feel more manageable to the legs. Fixed handles do
            the opposite job: leaning on them takes weight off the legs and cuts the real cost,
            exactly as holding a treadmill's rails does on an incline, while the console's figure
            stays the same.</li>
            <li><strong>No pacing pressure.</strong> On a treadmill the speed is set and you keep up.
            On an elliptical you set the pace with every stride, and when attention drifts —
            watching a show, reading a phone — most people slow down without noticing.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            None of that makes the elliptical a worse calorie burner in principle. It makes it a
            machine on which it is easy to do moderate work while believing you are doing vigorous
            work. The fix is to judge effort by your breathing rather than by how hard the motion
            feels: if you can talk in full sentences, it is moderate, whatever the resistance number
            says. A heart-rate monitor helps here too; our guide to
            <a href="/treadmill-heart-rate-zones/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">heart rate zones</a>
            applies to either machine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="consoles" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Why Do Console Calorie Figures Disagree and Run High?</h2>
<p>
            Because neither machine measures calories. Both estimate them from a few inputs and
            assumptions, and the assumptions tend to be generous.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A treadmill console</strong> typically applies a version of the standard walking
            and running equations to the belt speed and incline, multiplied by a body weight — often a
            default weight unless you enter yours. The equations assume you are walking or running
            hands-free on an accurately calibrated belt. <a href="/should-you-hold-the-handrails-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Hold the rails</a>, or use a machine whose speed
            or incline reads a little high, and the console keeps counting calories you did not burn.
            Many consoles also report gross calories, including the energy you would have used
            sitting down. Our guide to whether
            <a href="/are-treadmill-calories-accurate/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill calories are accurate</a>
            goes through each of these.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>An elliptical console</strong> has a harder job. It has no equivalent of a
            well-established speed-and-grade equation; it knows stride rate and a resistance level,
            and converts those into calories with a formula of the manufacturer's choosing. Resistance
            levels are not standardised between brands, stride lengths differ between machines, and
            momentum means that a fast stride at low resistance can look like hard work to the
            formula. If the console also assumes a default body weight, the error compounds.
          </p>
          <p>
            The result is that the two consoles are not measuring the same thing in the same way, so
            comparing an elliptical readout with a treadmill readout tells you very little. A higher
            number on the elliptical is at least as likely to reflect a generous formula as a harder
            workout. Wrist trackers and chest straps add a third and fourth opinion, with their own
            assumptions.
          </p>
          <p>
            The sensible approach is the same for both machines: enter your real weight wherever the
            console allows, keep your hands off fixed rails and handles, use one source consistently,
            and compare sessions only within that source. Treat any absolute figure as an estimate
            that probably runs high, and if you want an independent check, the MET arithmetic in the
            tables above is a fair one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="impact" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Impact and Joints: The Trade Behind the Numbers</h2>
<p>
            Calories are only half of this comparison, because the cheapest way to raise the burn on
            a treadmill — running — is also the one that adds impact. Each running stride lands with a
            peak vertical force commonly measured at around two to three times body weight, against
            roughly one to one and a half times body weight for walking. An elliptical removes the
            landing almost entirely.
          </p>
          <p>
            That has three practical consequences for anyone comparing the two on calories.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>If running hurts, the elliptical's "vigorous" band is the easiest place to
            reach running-level calorie burn</strong> without the impact. For people with sore knees,
            hips or feet, or who are heavy enough that running is uncomfortable, that is the
            elliptical's strongest case.</li>
            <li><strong>Treadmill walking is already low impact.</strong> Brisk or incline walking
            gets you into the same calorie band as moderate elliptical work with no more impact than
            ordinary walking. If you can walk comfortably, the treadmill does not force you to run to
            burn a useful amount.</li>
            <li><strong>Impact is not purely a cost.</strong> Bone responds to loading, and the
            jolts the elliptical removes are part of what makes running and brisk walking good for
            bone. The elliptical remains weight-bearing and better than nothing, but it is a weaker
            stimulus.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            The elliptical also has a fixed stride path, which suits some joints and aggravates
            others. If you have a specific joint problem, a physical therapist's advice on which
            motion suits you is worth more than any calorie table.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="weight-loss" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. What Does "Burns More" Mean for Weight Loss?</h2>
<p>
            Less than the question implies. The gap between the two machines at matched effort is,
            for a 160 lb person, somewhere around 5 to 10 calories per half hour — less than a mouthful
            of most snacks. Even the bigger gap, between a moderate elliptical session and a 30-minute
            run, is around 160 net calories: real, but modest.
          </p>
          <p>
            And exercise itself is a weaker lever for weight loss than most people expect. Studies of
            exercise programs without dietary change generally find modest weight loss, smaller than
            the calories burned would predict, partly because appetite tends to rise and partly
            because people tend to move a little less for the rest of the day after a hard session.
            Diet has the larger effect on the scales; exercise matters a great deal for fitness,
            health, mood and keeping weight off once it is lost.
          </p>
          <p>
            For weight loss, that suggests a few honest priorities:
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Pick the machine you will use most weeks.</strong> Consistency over months
            dwarfs any per-session difference.</li>
            <li><strong>Work at the hardest level you can sustain comfortably and safely</strong>,
            and be honest with yourself about it — the talk test is a better guide than the
            console.</li>
            <li><strong>Do not eat back the console figure.</strong> It probably runs high on either
            machine, and it is gross rather than net.</li>
            <li><strong>Look to diet for the bigger change</strong>, and to a registered dietitian
            for advice tailored to you.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            Our guide to
            <a href="/treadmill-workout-for-weight-loss/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill workouts for weight loss</a>
            sets out what actually helps, including why the "fat-burning zone" matters less than
            total work, and our
            <a href="/treadmill-calories-burned/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill calorie estimates</a>
            cover more speeds and inclines than this page does.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which-to-use" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. So Which Should You Use?</h2>
<p>
            On calories alone, the honest answer is whichever you will work harder on, more often.
            Beyond that, a few situations point clearly one way.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>You want the highest burn per minute and can run comfortably:</strong> the
            treadmill. Running at 6 to 7 mph and above, or intervals, goes beyond the elliptical's
            vigorous rating.</li>
            <li><strong>Running hurts, or impact is a problem for you or your downstairs
            neighbours:</strong> the elliptical, worked at a genuinely vigorous effort, comes close to
            running's burn without the impact.</li>
            <li><strong>You walk rather than run:</strong> a treadmill with a good incline range lets
            brisk incline walking reach the same band as moderate-to-hard elliptical work.</li>
            <li><strong>You tend to coast:</strong> the treadmill's fixed speed is harder to fake; if
            you use an elliptical, judge effort by breathing and a heart-rate monitor, not the motion.</li>
            <li><strong>You are training for running events:</strong> the treadmill, because only
            running trains running. The elliptical is useful cross-training around it.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            For everything else that decides between the two — footprint, ceiling height, noise,
            maintenance, bone loading and cost — see our full
            <a href="/treadmill-vs-elliptical/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill vs elliptical</a>
            comparison. If you have decided on an elliptical, our roundup of the
            <a href="/best-elliptical-machines-for-home/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best elliptical machines for home</a>
            covers stride length, ceiling height and current models across the price range.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What burns more calories, an elliptical or a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Running on a treadmill burns slightly more than vigorous elliptical work, and a moderate elliptical session burns about what a brisk treadmill walk does. The 2024 Compendium of Physical Activities rates elliptical training at 5.0 METs moderate and 9.0 vigorous, against 4.8 for walking at 3.5 to 3.9 mph and 9.3 for running at 6 mph. At matched effort, the difference is small.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many calories does 30 minutes on an elliptical burn?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Using 2024 Compendium values, roughly 150 to 230 calories at moderate effort and 265 to 410 at vigorous effort for people weighing about 130 to 200 lb. These are estimates from calories = METs x kg x hours, not measurements, and they include the energy you would have used at rest.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does the elliptical show more calories than the treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Elliptical consoles estimate calories from stride rate and a resistance level using the manufacturer's own formula, often with a default body weight. Resistance is not standardised between brands and momentum can make easy strides look like hard work. A higher elliptical number is at least as likely to reflect a generous formula as a harder workout.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the elliptical or the treadmill better for weight loss?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Neither is clearly better. The calorie difference at matched effort is small, and exercise alone tends to produce modest weight loss because appetite and daily movement compensate. Choose the machine you will use most consistently at a genuinely hard effort, do not eat back console figures, and look to diet for the larger change.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does incline walking burn as much as the elliptical?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Brisk walking at a 6 to 10% grade is rated at about 7.0 METs in the 2024 Compendium, which is more than moderate elliptical work at 5.0 and less than vigorous elliptical work at 9.0. For a 160 lb person that is roughly 255 calories per half hour, without running's impact.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does the elliptical feel easier than running?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                There is no landing impact, the flywheel's momentum carries the pedals between pushes, and moving handles share the work with the arms. Those make the same effort feel smoother, and they also make it easy to slow down without noticing. Judge elliptical effort by your breathing or heart rate rather than by how the motion feels.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes When Comparing Elliptical and Treadmill Calories</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Most of the confusion in this comparison comes from trusting the consoles or misjudging effort, not from the machines themselves.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing the two consoles directly</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">An elliptical and a treadmill estimate calories with different formulas and assumptions, so their readouts are not comparable. Compare sessions only within one machine or one device, enter your real weight, and use MET arithmetic from a published source as an independent check on either number.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mistaking a smooth stride for a hard workout</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Momentum and the absence of impact make elliptical work feel easier than it looks, and low resistance with a fast stride can be quite light work. If you can talk in full sentences, it is moderate effort. Raise the resistance until your breathing, not the stride rate, says you are working.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaning on fixed handles or treadmill rails</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Supporting your weight through fixed handles on an elliptical, or the rails on an incline treadmill, takes load off the legs and lowers the real calorie cost while the console keeps counting. Use the moving handles or none, and lower the incline or resistance rather than hold on.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing a machine by a small calorie difference</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">At matched effort the gap is a few percent, far smaller than the difference between a session done and one skipped. Pick the machine whose motion, impact and space suit you, the one you will use most weeks, and look to diet rather than machine choice for the bigger effect on weight.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

