        <!-- Closing CTA -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">A treadmill burns slightly more than an elliptical at the top end,
            because running outscores even vigorous elliptical work, but at matched effort the two are
            within a few percent.</strong> Using 2024 Compendium values, a 160 lb person burns roughly
            180 calories in 30 moderate elliptical minutes, 330 in 30 vigorous ones and 340 running at
            6 mph — estimates, not measurements.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Both consoles tend to overstate the burn, and the elliptical makes it easy to coast without
            noticing. Judge effort by your breathing, choose the machine you will use hard and often,
            and remember that diet moves the scales more than either. For the rest of the decision,
            see our full
            <a href="/treadmill-vs-elliptical/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">treadmill vs elliptical comparison</a>.
